```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__pixellab__edit_image(args: {
  // What to change (e.g. 'add a red wizard hat', 'make the armor gold'). Required unless reference_image_base64 is given.
  description?: string | null;
  // Output height. Defaults to the first input image's height.
  height?: number | null;
  // Alternative to images_base64: https, data: or `upload:<id>` (POST /v2/uploads with this same bearer token) URLs to the PNG(s) to edit, in the same one-per-frame list form. PREFER THIS for anything but tiny sprites. Give urls OR base64, not both.
  image_urls?: Array<string> | null;
  // Base64 PNG(s) to edit — pass a one-element list for a single image. Several frames get the SAME edit applied consistently (useful for animation frames or a character's directions). Max 512x512 each. PREFER image_urls — MCP clients routinely truncate large inline base64.
  images_base64?: Array<string> | null;
  // Background handling. Default (unset) follows the inputs: if any frame has transparency they all stay transparent, otherwise they stay opaque. Pass False to flatten a transparent input onto white.
  no_background?: boolean | null;
  // Internal Game Builder project context; the finished image is linked after generation.
  project_id?: string | null;
  // Base64 PNG to copy the appearance of (outfit/colors/details) onto the input image, keeping its pose. Switches the tool into reference mode; description then just refines the instruction. To put an outfit on ANIMATION frames, use transfer_outfit instead.
  reference_image_base64?: string | null;
  // Alternative to reference_image_base64: an https, data: or `upload:<id>` URL to the reference PNG. PREFER THIS for anything but a tiny sprite. Give one, not both.
  reference_image_url?: string | null;
  // Seed for reproducible generation.
  seed?: number | null;
  // Output width. Defaults to the first input image's width.
  width?: number | null;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

Edit an image with a text instruction on the Pixen model (fast, 1 generation).

Returns YOUR image edited — pose, composition and pixel style are preserved
and only what you asked for changes.

Pick between the two edit tools:
- edit_image_pixen — this one; 1 generation, ~10-40s, ONE frame, input max
  256px per side and output max 256x256 in AREA. The default for a single
  sprite.
- edit_image — pro quality, 10-25 generations, ~30-90s, edits up to 16
  frames consistently in one call, accepts a reference image and inputs up
  to 512px. Worth it for animation frames, a character's directions, or when
  pixen's result isn't good enough.

Sizes: the input is capped PER SIDE at 256 (crop an oversized source —
never rescale pixel art); the output is capped by AREA at 256x256 =
65,536px, so 128x512 is fine but 320x320 is not. Output defaults to the
input's size. BOTH sides of both must be multiples of 4 — a 63x222 sprite
is rejected; pad it with transparent pixels to 64x224 first (padding keeps
the pixel grid, rescaling destroys it). Need a bigger canvas? edit_image
takes inputs up to 512.

Cost: 1 generation. Generation time: ~10-40 seconds (async).
Poll with get_image(job_id).

